    "q-101941": {
      "dom_id": "step-q101941",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{7-3x}{2}>2.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7-3x}{2} & >2 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{7-3x}{2}) & >2\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot (\\frac{7-3x}{\\cancel{2}}) & >2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 7-3x & >4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-3x & >4 \\\\ 7-3x-7 & >4-7 \\\\ -3x & >-3 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3} & \\,\\,<\\,\\,\\frac{-3}{-3} \\\\ x & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
