    "q-181417": {
      "dom_id": "step-q181417",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{x+6}{4}>\\frac{2x}{3}.$\n\nThe solution is $x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+6}{4} & >\\frac{2x}{3} \\\\ 12\\cdot (\\frac{x+6}{4}) & >12\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{3}) \\\\ 3\\cdot (x+6) & >4\\cdot 2x \\\\ 3x+18 & >8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+18 & >8x \\\\ 3x+18-8x & >8x-8x \\\\ 18-5x & >0 \\\\ 18-5x-18 & >0-18 \\\\ -5x & >-18 \\\\ \\frac{-5x}{-5} & \\,\\,<\\,\\,\\frac{-18}{-5} \\\\ x & <\\frac{18}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<\\frac{18}{5}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
